WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
The reflection property replaces x by a plus b minus x without changing the value of the integral.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Add the original and reflected forms; a symmetric denominator often collapses to a constant.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.000
This is why the king property is powerful for sine and cosine over a full symmetric interval.

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
When you substitute, change the limits immediately. Old limits with a new variable are a common error.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.000
If t equals a plus b minus x, then dt equals minus dx and the limits swap.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.000
The two minus signs cancel when you reorder the limits, leaving the integral unchanged.

7
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Symmetry gives a shortcut: the integral of an odd function over minus a to a is zero.

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Check oddness about the centre of the interval, not only about the origin.

9
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Split the integrand into symmetric and antisymmetric parts before doing heavy algebra.

10
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Practice prompt: integrate x over minus two to two, then integrate x squared over the same interval.

11
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.000
The first is zero by odd symmetry; the second is twice the integral from zero to two.

12
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Write both answers with a one-line reason, then take the end check.
